This PR adds a test case that checks that a switch to tx relay via `filterclear` can trigger eviction. 

It kills the following mutant (https://mutanthub.space/mutants/3746):
```diff
diff --git a/src/net_processing.cpp b/src/net_processing.cpp
index 96caf9050f..d9019db877 100644
--- a/src/net_processing.cpp
+++ b/src/net_processing.cpp
@@ -5312,7 +5312,6 @@ void PeerManagerImpl::ProcessMessage(Peer& peer, CNode& pfrom, const std::string
         }
         pfrom.m_bloom_filter_loaded = false;
         pfrom.m_relays_txs = true;
-        MaybeDisconnectForTxRelayCapacity(pfrom, msg_type);
         return;
     }

```

The `check_slow_read_throttle` functional test may intermittently fail on slow build configs (like sanitizers+debug+no-opt). This is fine, but it would be nice if it passed.

To reproduce the failure, add a long sleep for the big json requests:

```diff
diff --git a/src/httpserver.cpp b/src/httpserver.cpp
index 0c25ebbba5..42c54c2cdf 100644
--- a/src/httpserver.cpp
+++ b/src/httpserver.cpp
@@ -614,2 +614,3 @@ void HTTPRemoteClient::Send(const HTTPResponse& res, std::span<const std::byte>
     bool send_buffer_was_empty{false};
+    bool send_buffer_over_limit{false};
     // Fill the send buffer with the complete serialized response headers + body
@@ -634,2 +635,4 @@ void HTTPRemoteClient::Send(const HTTPResponse& res, std::span<const std::byte>
         if (!send_buffer_was_empty) m_send_ready = true;
+
+        send_buffer_over_limit = m_send_buffer.size() > MAX_BODY_SIZE;
     }
@@ -652,2 +655,11 @@ void HTTPRemoteClient::Send(const HTTPResponse& res, std::span<const std::byte>
 
+    // TEST-ONLY: Widen the interval between queueing a throttling-sized response
+    // and allowing the next pipelined request to be dispatched. This reproduces
+    // the slow-worker scheduling window exercised by interface_http.py.
+    if (send_buffer_over_limit) {
+    // The functional test waits twice for five seconds before it starts
+    // its own ten-second recv() timeout, so this must cover both phases.
+    std::this_thread::sleep_for(30s);
+    }
+
     // Signal to the I/O loop that we are ready to handle the next request.
```

Then, on master (or on this pull with `--timeout-factor=1`), it will fail and with `--timeout-factor=3` it will pass:

```
./bld-cmake/test/functional/interface_http.py --test_methods check_slow_read_throttle --loglevel=DEBUG --timeout-factor 3
```

The C++ standard library lacks a type to denote a smart pointer is not null. For raw pointer there is `std::reference_wrapper`, or a plain reference.

Other third-party libraries provide such a type, such as `gsl::strict_not_null`.

Fix all issues by adding `util::NotNull<SmartPtrType>`, which documents (and checks) that the inner pointer is never null.

This type can be used when passing never-null smart pointers between functions. It removes the need to `Assert()` the pointer before dereference. For example, in a getter function:

```cpp
util::NotNull<std::unique_ptr<Stats>> GetStats()
{
    return util::NotNull{std::make_unique<Stats>()};
}

int main()
{
    auto stats{GetStats()};
    stats->foo; // This can never lead to a nullptr deref
    // Assert(stats)->foo; // This is redundant and won't compile
}
```

Fixes https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/issues/24423

Kills some live mutants on `interpreter.cpp` found by https://bitcoincore.space. They are:

[interpreter.cpp#5168](https://bitcoincore.space/src/script/interpreter.cpp#5168): `IsCompressedOrUncompressedPubKey`: pubkey with neither compressed nor uncompressed prefix returns `true`

```diff
-    } else if (vchPubKey[0] == 0x02 || vchPubKey[0] == 0x03) {
+    } else if (1==1) {
```

[interpreter.cpp#5191](https://bitcoincore.space/src/script/interpreter.cpp#5191): `IsValidSignatureEncoding`: 73-byte upper bound returns `true`

```diff
     if (sig.size() < 9) return false;
-    if (sig.size() > 73) return false;
```

[interpreter.cpp#5200](https://bitcoincore.space/src/script/interpreter.cpp#5200): `IsValidSignatureEncoding`: total-length byte check returns `true`

```diff 
     // Make sure the length covers the entire signature.
-    if (sig[1] != sig.size() - 3) return false;
```

[interpreter.cpp#5232](https://bitcoincore.space/src/script/interpreter.cpp#5232): `IsValidSignatureEncoding`: null R byte check `lenR > 1` -> `lenR >=1`

```diff
     // Null bytes at the start of R are not allowed, unless R would
     // otherwise be interpreted as a negative number.
-    if (lenR > 1 && (sig[4] == 0x00) && !(sig[5] & 0x80)) return false;
+    if (lenR >= 1 && (sig[4] == 0x00) && !(sig[5] & 0x80)) return false;
```

[interpreter.cpp#5259](https://bitcoincore.space/src/script/interpreter.cpp#5259): `IsValidSignatureEncoding`: null S byte check `lenS > 1` -> `lenS >= 1`

```diff
     // Null bytes at the start of S are not allowed, unless S would otherwise be
     // interpreted as a negative number.
-    if (lenS > 1 && (sig[lenR + 6] == 0x00) && !(sig[lenR + 7] & 0x80)) return false;
+    if (lenS >= 1 && (sig[lenR + 6] == 0x00) && !(sig[lenR + 7] & 0x80)) return false;
```

[interpreter.cpp#5186](https://bitcoincore.space/src/script/interpreter.cpp#5186): `IsValidSignatureEncoding`: 9-byte lower bound returns `true`

```diff 
     // Minimum and maximum size constraints.
-    if (sig.size() < 9) return false;
```

The first affects policy only, the next four affect consensus, and the last covers the lower bound and lets ASan catch UB: a 3-byte signature reaches the `sig[3]` read that the `sig.size() < 9` guard protects.

Recommend reviewing per commit.
